---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { getPathwayCategoryHero } from '@/lib/pathwayHeroes';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title = 'EB-5 for Families With Children Aging Out | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 for families with children aging out explains the CSPA-related risk of dependents turning 21 under a parent’s petition, including H-4 and India-backlog contexts (without case-specific age math).';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-child-aging-out`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 for Families With Children Aging Out';
const subhead =
  'Why some parents evaluate EB-5 when a child’s eligibility under a pending green card case is tied to turning 21.';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'EB-5 for H-4 Families & Dependents',
    url: '/pathways/eb5-for-h4-families',
    description: 'How a primary investor’s petition can include a spouse and unmarried children under 21.',
  },
  {
    title: 'EB-5 Investment Process',
    url: '/eb5-investment-process',
    description: 'Step-by-step filing sequence from source of funds through I-829.',
  },
];
const hero = getPathwayCategoryHero('process-mechanics');

---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroImageWebp={hero.webp}
  heroImageAlt="EB-5 pathway addressing EB-5 child aging out"
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About EB-5 and Dependent Children"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 Child Aging Out"
  keywords={[
    'EB-5 child aging out',
    'CSPA EB-5 families',
    'H-4 children aging out',
    'India backlog aging out',
  ]}
>
  <h2>The aging-out problem, in plain language</h2>
  <p>
    Under many family and employment-based immigrant petitions, unmarried children can be included as derivatives only
    while they remain under 21 (with limited statutory protections). The Child Status Protection Act (CSPA) can, in some
    cases, preserve a child's age for immigration purposes, but CSPA formulas are fact-specific and depend on petition
    type, filing and approval dates, and visa availability. This page does not calculate CSPA age for any child. If a
    dependent is approaching 21 while a parent's case is still waiting for a visa number, families often look for paths
    that can lock in derivative status earlier or on a different category timeline. EB-5 is one option some households
    evaluate because the child can be included as a derivative on the investor's petition when eligibility rules are
    met. Confirm your child's situation with an immigration attorney before relying on any strategy.
  </p>

  <h2>H-4 children and aging out</h2>
  <p>
    Children on H-4 status as dependents of an H-1B parent face a version of the same clock: their ability to stay in
    the U.S. as H-4s, and to remain derivatives on a parent's immigrant petition, is tied to age and to the parent's
    continuing status and green card case. When the parent's employment-based wait stretches, an H-4 child can age out
    of derivative eligibility even if the family has lived in the U.S. for years. Some H-1B households evaluate an EB-5
    filing so a spouse and unmarried children under 21 can be included on the investor's petition as derivatives. See
    <a href="/pathways/eb5-for-h4-families">EB-5 for H-4 families &amp; dependents</a>. That structure still requires
    attorney review of CSPA and derivative eligibility for each child; it is not automatic protection against aging out.
  </p>

  <h2>Aging out and the India backlog</h2>
  <p>
    India's EB-2 and EB-3 employment-based categories have historically faced significant demand under per-country
    limits. Longer queues mean more calendar time between a parent's early petition steps and the moment a visa number
    becomes available, and that calendar time is exactly what interacts with a child's age. Indian families therefore
    encounter aging-out pressure more often and more acutely than families from most other countries of birth, even when
    the parent's case is otherwise strong. Exact wait times change; use the
    <a
      href="https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/legal/visa-law0/visa-bulletin.html"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">Visa Bulletin</a
    > and an attorney for current category movement. India-focused pathway context:
    <a href="/pathways/eb5-for-indian-investors">EB-5 for Indian investors</a> and
    <a href="/pathways/eb5-for-indian-h1b">EB-5 for Indian H-1B professionals</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <p>
    For the investment and filing sequence itself, see the
    <a href="/eb5-investment-process">EB-5 investment process</a>. For any CSPA calculation or dual-track strategy
    involving a specific child, speak with an immigration attorney. Do not use this page as a substitute for that
    analysis.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
